      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 42px); margin-bottom: 20px;">Either…Or, Neither…Nor, Not Only…But Also: The Proximity Rule Made Easy with Solved Examples</h1>
      <p style="font-size: 16px; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 28px; line-height: 1.65;">
        Understand the proximity rule for either…or, neither…nor and not only…but also, with solved examples and practice questions for exam success.
      </p>

      <div style="padding: 16px 20px; border-radius: var(--radius-lg); background-color: var(--color-primary-subtle); border: 1px solid var(--color-primary-border); margin-bottom: 36px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; font-weight: 800; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 4px;">Core Governing Rule Formula:</div>
        <div style="font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-primary);">Either...or / Neither...nor / Not only...but also = verb agrees with the NEARER subject.</div>
      </div>

      <div style="font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 48px;">
        <img src="https://bkandekar.github.io/ZeroErrorEnglishPro/images/proximity-rule-either-or-neither-nor.webp" alt="Proximity rule for either or, neither nor and not only but also with solved examples" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px"><p>&ldquo;Neither the teacher nor the students <i>was</i> present.&rdquo; Right or wrong? If you hesitated, you're in good company. When two subjects are joined by <b>or</b> or <b>nor</b>, even strong students wonder which subject gets to choose the verb.</p><p>Here's the good news: there's a simple rule, called the <b>proximity rule</b>, and it works the same way every time. The verb agrees with the subject <b>closest to it</b>. That's it. No exceptions to memorise, no list of nouns to learn.</p><p>In this guide, you'll see which connectors follow the rule, which words don't count as subjects, what happens when the subjects have different persons, and how the word and behaves differently. You'll also get six solved examples, ten exam traps in one table and a ten-sentence practice set with answers.</p><div style="background:#f6f4ee;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;border-radius:6px;padding:12px 18px;margin:18px 0;color:#1c2430"><b>In this guide</b><ol style="margin:8px 0 0"><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('why').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Why Two Subjects Create So Much Confusion</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('rule').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">The Proximity Rule</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('conn').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">The Connectors at a Glance</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('either').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Either...Or and Neither...Nor</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('notonly').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Not Only...But Also and Not...But</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('person').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">When the Subjects Have Different Persons: I, You, He</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('and').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">What About And?</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('ignore').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Words That Do NOT Count as Subjects</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('swap').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">The Swap Trick: Same Words, Different Verb</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('long').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Proximity in Longer Sentences</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('walk').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Six Solved Examples</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('mist').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">5 Mistakes Students Make With Proximity</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('table').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">10 Exam Traps at a Glance</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('case').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Case Study: Two Questions, Two Different Rules</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('practice').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Practice: Spot the Error</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('tips').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">4 Tips From the Classroom</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('summary').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">One-Page Revision Summary</a></li><li style="margin:4px 0"><a href="javascript:void(0)" onclick="document.getElementById('faq').scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth'})">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li></ol></div><h2 id="why" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Why Two Subjects Create So Much Confusion</h2><p>In a simple sentence such as &ldquo;The boy is tall&rdquo;, there's one subject and one verb. Life is easy. But the moment two subjects appear, the verb has to make a choice. Should it go singular because of the first subject, or plural because of the second?</p><p>Our daily speech gives us no help. People say &ldquo;Neither the teacher nor the students were present&rdquo; and &ldquo;Neither the teacher nor the students was present&rdquo; without noticing the difference. Examiners, however, notice it, and they build questions on it.</p><p>The proximity rule gives you a way to decide without guessing. It also helps you spot the connectors where the rule does not apply, and that's where the bigger traps hide.</p><h2 id="rule" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">The Proximity Rule</h2><div style="border-left:5px solid #C9A227;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>The Rule</b><br>With <b>either...or, neither...nor, not only...but also, or, nor</b> and <b>not...but</b>, the verb agrees with the subject <b>nearest to it</b>.</div><div style="border-left:5px solid #C9A227;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>Memory trick: the Nearest Neighbour</b><br>The verb listens only to its next-door neighbour. Whoever stands closest to the verb decides singular or plural.</div><h2 id="conn" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">The Connectors at a Glance</h2><div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:15px"><tr><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Connector</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Example</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Nearer subject</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either...or</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the manager or the clerks <b>are</b> responsible.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">clerks (plural)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither...nor</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither the students nor the teacher <b>was</b> present.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">teacher (singular)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only...but also</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only the manager but also his assistants <b>are</b> responsible.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">assistants (plural)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Or</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The captain or the players <b>are</b> to blame.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">players (plural)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not...but</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not the teacher but the students <b>are</b> late.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">students (plural)</td></tr></table></div><p>In every row, the verb looks at the nearer subject and ignores the other one. The first subject, no matter how important it sounds, plays no role in choosing the verb.</p><h2 id="either" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Either...Or and Neither...Nor</h2><p>Both connectors work the same way, and both follow the nearest subject. Two singular subjects take a singular verb, two plural subjects take a plural verb, and a mix follows the nearer one.</p><ul><li>Either the manager or the clerk <b>is</b> responsible. (Both singular.)</li><li>Neither the students nor the teachers <b>were</b> present. (Both plural.)</li><li>Neither the teacher nor the students <b>were</b> present. (Nearer: students.)</li><li>Neither the students nor the teacher <b>was</b> present. (Nearer: teacher.)</li></ul><p>Look at the last two. The same words appear in both, only in a different order. That's the favourite trick of examiners, because a student who guesses by the first subject gets one wrong.</p><h2 id="notonly" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Not Only...But Also and Not...But</h2><p><b>Not only...but also</b> follows the same rule. Don't be distracted by the importance of the first part. The verb belongs to the subject after but also.</p><ul><li>Not only the manager but also his assistants <b>are</b> responsible.</li><li>Not only the assistants but also the manager <b>is</b> responsible.</li><li>Not the teacher but the students <b>are</b> late.</li></ul><p>The pattern <b>not...but</b> is slightly rarer, but it follows the same logic. The verb agrees with the subject after but.</p><h2 id="person" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">When the Subjects Have Different Persons: I, You, He</h2><p>What if one subject is I and the other is you or he? The nearest-subject rule still applies, and the verb takes the form that matches the nearer pronoun.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:15px"><tr><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Sentence</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Why</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either you or I <b>am</b> to blame.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject: I. So am.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either I or you <b>are</b> to blame.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject: you. So are.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither he nor I <b>am</b> ready.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject: I. So am.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither you nor he <b>is</b> ready.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject: he. So is.</td></tr></table></div><p>These sentences sound clumsy, so in your own writing, you can rearrange them: &ldquo;Either you are to blame, or I am.&rdquo; But in an exam, you'll meet the clumsy versions, and the nearest subject always wins.</p><h2 id="and" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">What About And?</h2><p>The word <b>and</b> is different, because and adds. Two subjects joined by and take a plural verb: &ldquo;Ravi and Amit <b>are</b> friends.&rdquo; &ldquo;Both Ravi and Amit <b>are</b> present.&rdquo;</p><p>There are two exceptions you must know:</p><ol><li><b>One idea, two words:</b> &ldquo;Bread and butter <b>is</b> my usual breakfast.&rdquo; &ldquo;Rice and curry <b>is</b> my favourite meal.&rdquo; When the two words form a single dish or idea, the verb is singular.</li><li><b>One person, two roles:</b> &ldquo;The poet and novelist <b>was</b> honoured.&rdquo; Here the article the appears only once, so one person has both roles. But &ldquo;The poet and the novelist <b>were</b> honoured&rdquo; names two persons, so the verb is plural.</li></ol><p>And remember the other exception from <a href="#post/each-every-either-neither-singular-verb">my guide to each and every</a>: &ldquo;Each boy and each girl <b>has</b> a prize.&rdquo; When each or every comes before both nouns, the verb is singular.</p><h2 id="ignore" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Words That Do NOT Count as Subjects</h2><p>These phrases add extra information. They don't add a subject. Ignore them when you choose the verb:</p><ul><li><b>along with</b>: The captain, along with his players, <b>is</b> arriving.</li><li><b>as well as</b>: The principal, as well as the teachers, <b>is</b> attending.</li><li><b>together with</b>: The manager, together with his staff, <b>has</b> left.</li><li><b>in addition to, besides, with</b>: The teacher, in addition to the students, <b>was</b> invited.</li></ul><p>In these sentences, the verb agrees with the <b>first</b> subject. This is a different rule from proximity, and mixing them up is a very common mistake. Proximity applies to or and nor. Ignoring applies to along with and as well as.</p><div style="border-left:5px solid #0B1F3A;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>Exam Note</b><br>Compare &ldquo;The captain <b>and</b> his players <b>are</b> arriving&rdquo; with &ldquo;The captain, <b>along with</b> his players, <b>is</b> arriving&rdquo;. One tiny word changes the verb.</div><h2 id="swap" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">The Swap Trick: Same Words, Different Verb</h2><p>Here's a trick I use in class. Take any proximity sentence, swap the order of the two subjects, and watch the verb change. If the verb follows the nearer subject each time, you've understood the rule.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:15px"><tr><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Original</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Swapped</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither the teacher nor the students <b>were</b> present.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither the students nor the teacher <b>was</b> present.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the coach or the players <b>are</b> responsible.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the players or the coach <b>is</b> responsible.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only the manager but also his assistants <b>are</b> late.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only his assistants but also the manager <b>is</b> late.</td></tr></table></div><p>Practise this swap on ten sentences from your textbook. It trains your eye to look at the nearer subject without thinking.</p><h2 id="long" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Proximity in Longer Sentences</h2><p>In real exam questions, the two subjects are rarely short. Examiners stretch the sentence with extra phrases, and the nearer subject can be hard to spot.</p><div style="border-left:5px solid #0B1F3A;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>Example</b><br>Neither the chairman of the committee nor the members of the board who attended the meeting <b>were</b> aware of the changes.</div><p>The nearer subject is <b>members</b>, which is plural, so the verb is were. The phrases of the committee, of the board and who attended the meeting are decoys. Here's how to handle long sentences:</p><ol><li>Find the connector first (neither...nor).</li><li>Find the verb and walk <b>backwards</b> from it until you reach the first subject.</li><li>Strike out any of-phrase or who-phrase attached to that subject.</li><li>Check whether the subject is singular or plural.</li></ol><p>Working backwards from the verb is the quickest way to find the nearer subject, because the nearer subject is, by definition, the one you reach first.</p><p>In your own writing, try to avoid stretched either...or sentences. Splitting them into two shorter sentences usually reads better and removes the risk of a verb error altogether.</p><h2 id="walk" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Six Solved Examples</h2><ol><li><b>Either the principal or the teachers __ going to decide.</b> Nearer: teachers. Answer: <b>are</b>.</li><li><b>Neither the boys nor the girl __ selected.</b> Nearer: girl. Answer: <b>was</b>.</li><li><b>Not only the leader but also his followers __ guilty.</b> Nearer: followers. Answer: <b>are</b>.</li><li><b>The coach, as well as the players, __ happy.</b> As well as doesn't count. Answer: <b>is</b>.</li><li><b>Either he or I __ responsible.</b> Nearer: I. Answer: <b>am</b>.</li><li><b>Bread and butter __ his favourite breakfast.</b> One idea. Answer: <b>is</b>.</li></ol><p>Cover the answers and solve them first. If you got five out of six, you're ready for the exam pattern.</p><h2 id="mist" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">5 Mistakes Students Make With Proximity</h2><ol><li><b>Choosing by the first subject.</b> The verb follows the nearer subject, not the first.</li><li><b>Treating as well as like and.</b> As well as does not join subjects. Ignore it.</li><li><b>Missing the person.</b> With I and you, the verb form follows the nearer pronoun: am, are or is.</li><li><b>Forgetting the exceptions to and.</b> Bread and butter and the poet and novelist are single-idea cases.</li><li><b>Assuming plural means safe.</b> Some students pick the plural verb every time because it feels safer. Check the nearer subject instead.</li></ol><h2 id="table" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">10 Exam Traps at a Glance</h2><div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:15px"><tr><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Wrong</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Correct</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the coach or the players is responsible.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the coach or the players <b>are</b> responsible.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither she nor her brothers was invited.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither she nor her brothers <b>were</b> invited.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only the students but also the teacher were late.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only the students but also the teacher <b>was</b> late.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The principal as well as the teachers are attending.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The principal as well as the teachers <b>is</b> attending.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either you or he are to blame.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either you or he <b>is</b> to blame.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither the manager nor his assistants has arrived.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither the manager nor his assistants <b>have</b> arrived.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The poet and novelist were honoured.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The poet and novelist <b>was</b> honoured.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Ravi, along with his friends, are coming.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Ravi, along with his friends, <b>is</b> coming.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only the leader but also his followers is guilty.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only the leader but also his followers <b>are</b> guilty.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the teachers or the principal are going to decide.</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Either the teachers or the principal <b>is</b> going to decide.</td></tr></table></div><h2 id="case" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Case Study: Two Questions, Two Different Rules</h2><p>Here are two SSC CGL exam pattern practice questions. Each uses a different rule.</p><div style="border-left:5px solid #0B1F3A;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>Question 1</b><br>Neither the teacher (A) / nor the students (B) / was present. (C) / No error (D)</div><p>The nearer subject is students, which is plural. The verb should be <b>were</b>. The error is in <b>C</b>.</p><div style="border-left:5px solid #0B1F3A;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>Question 2</b><br>The captain along with (A) / his players (B) / are arriving. (C) / No error (D)</div><p>Along with doesn't join subjects, so the verb follows captain. It should be <b>is arriving</b>. The error is again in <b>C</b>, but this time the reason is different. That's why you must identify the connector first.</p><h2 id="practice" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Practice: Spot the Error</h2><p>Find the error in each sentence before you read the answers. These are practice sentences in the common exam pattern, not year-tagged past questions.</p><ol><li>Either the coach or the players is responsible.</li><li>Neither she nor her brothers was invited.</li><li>Not only the students but also the teacher were late.</li><li>The principal as well as the teachers are attending the meeting.</li><li>Either you or he are to blame.</li><li>Neither the manager nor his assistants has arrived.</li><li>The poet and novelist were honoured at the function.</li><li>Ravi, along with his friends, are coming to the party.</li><li>Not only the leader but also his followers is guilty.</li><li>Either the teachers or the principal are going to decide.</li></ol><div style="border-left:5px solid #0B1F3A;background:rgba(201,162,39,.10);padding:12px 16px;margin:16px 0;border-radius:4px"><b>Answers</b><br><ol><li>&ldquo;is&rdquo; should be <b>are</b>. Nearer subject: players.</li><li>&ldquo;was&rdquo; should be <b>were</b>. Nearer subject: brothers.</li><li>&ldquo;were&rdquo; should be <b>was</b>. Nearer subject: teacher.</li><li>&ldquo;are&rdquo; should be <b>is</b>. As well as doesn't count.</li><li>&ldquo;are&rdquo; should be <b>is</b>. Nearer subject: he.</li><li>&ldquo;has&rdquo; should be <b>have</b>. Nearer subject: assistants.</li><li>&ldquo;were&rdquo; should be <b>was</b>. One person has both roles.</li><li>&ldquo;are&rdquo; should be <b>is</b>. Along with doesn't count.</li><li>&ldquo;is&rdquo; should be <b>are</b>. Nearer subject: followers.</li><li>&ldquo;are&rdquo; should be <b>is</b>. Nearer subject: principal.</li></ol></div><h2 id="tips" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">4 Tips From the Classroom</h2><h3 style="color:#0B1F3A;margin-top:22px">Tip 1: Identify the connector first</h3><p>Is it or, nor, either...or or not only...but also? Then use proximity. Is it along with or as well as? Then ignore the extra words.</p><h3 style="color:#0B1F3A;margin-top:22px">Tip 2: Mark the nearer subject</h3><p>Underline the subject closest to the verb before you do anything else.</p><h3 style="color:#0B1F3A;margin-top:22px">Tip 3: Practise the swap</h3><p>Swap the two subjects on ten sentences. If the verb changes accordingly, you've got the rule.</p><h3 style="color:#0B1F3A;margin-top:22px">Tip 4: Check the article</h3><p>With and, look at the article. The poet and novelist is one person. The poet and the novelist are two.</p><h2 id="summary" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">One-Page Revision Summary</h2><div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:15px"><tr><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Connector</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Rule</th><th style="background:#0B1F3A;color:#fff;padding:8px;text-align:left;border:1px solid #d9d4c0">Example</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">or, nor</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The captain or the players <b>are</b> to blame.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">either...or, neither...nor</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Neither he nor I <b>am</b> ready.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">not only...but also</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Nearer subject</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Not only he but also his sons <b>are</b> present.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">along with, as well as, together with</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Ignore; use first subject</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">The captain, along with his players, <b>is</b> here.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">and</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Plural (except single idea)</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Ravi and Amit <b>are</b> friends.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">each...and each</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Singular</td><td style="padding:8px;border:1px solid #d9d4c0;vertical-align:top">Each boy and each girl <b>has</b> a prize.</td></tr></table></div><p>Compound subjects and quasi-conjunctions get a full unit in my book <a href="https://amzn.to/4k9MucG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spot the Error!</a>, with exam-calibrated questions. For the other agreement traps, read <a href="#post/subject-verb-agreement-mistakes-ssc-cgl">the 15 mistakes that cost marks in SSC CGL</a>.</p><h2 id="faq" style="color:#0B1F3A;border-bottom:3px solid #C9A227;padding-bottom:4px;margin-top:34px">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p><b>What if one subject is singular and one plural with &ldquo;and&rdquo;?</b><br>With and, both subjects count, so the verb is plural. The proximity rule applies only to or, nor, either...or, neither...nor, not only...but also and not...but.</p><p><b>Which verb do I use with &ldquo;either you or I&rdquo;?</b><br>Use the nearer subject: &ldquo;Either you or I am to blame.&rdquo; If the order is &ldquo;Either I or you&rdquo;, the nearer subject is you, so the verb is are.</p><p><b>Does the proximity rule apply to &ldquo;as well as&rdquo;?</b><br>No. Words like as well as, along with and together with don't change the subject. The verb agrees with the first subject.</p><p><b>Why do examiners swap the order of the subjects?</b><br>Because students who guess by the first subject get one version right and the swapped version wrong. It tests whether you really apply the proximity rule.</p><p><b>Is &ldquo;The poet and novelist was honoured&rdquo; correct?</b><br>Yes, if one person is both poet and novelist. With the article before each noun, &ldquo;The poet and the novelist were honoured&rdquo;, two persons are meant.</p><p><b>Does &ldquo;both...and&rdquo; follow proximity?</b><br>No. Both...and always takes a plural verb: &ldquo;Both Ravi and Amit are present.&rdquo;</p><p><b>How can I practise this rule quickly?</b><br>Take ten textbook sentences with or or nor, swap the subjects, and check that the verb changes with the nearer subject.</p><p><b>Final word.</b> Identify the connector, find the nearer subject, then choose the verb. Practise the swap trick for a week, and proximity will become automatic. For every agreement rule and 200+ exam-calibrated questions, <a href="https://amzn.to/4k9MucG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">get Spot the Error! on Amazon</a>.</p>
